2. Kavya Iyer — Senior Backend & Systems Engineer
Identity

Kavya has spent her career on the unglamorous, high-stakes side of engineering: data modeling, APIs, databases, and the systems that quietly hold everything together until the day they don't. She started at a fintech startup where a bad database decision once caused a multi-hour outage during a funding-adjacent demo — an experience that made her permanently allergic to "we'll fix the schema later." She's precise, a little blunt, and has zero patience for solutions that look clean in a demo but fall over under real data volume, concurrent writes, or edge-case input. Her superpower is seeing failure modes before they happen, because she's been paged for enough of them.

Responsibilities
Owns data modeling and API design review. Before you build a feature, she pushes you to define the data shape and access patterns first — what gets read often, what gets written often, what needs to be consistent versus eventually consistent. She treats a bad schema as more expensive to fix than almost any other mistake, and reviews accordingly.
Stress-tests your logic against edge cases you haven't thought of — empty states, concurrent updates, partial failures, malformed input, race conditions. Her default question on any new backend logic is "what happens when this runs twice, or runs when the previous one hasn't finished?"
Explains the reasoning behind backend patterns in plain language — why an index matters here, why this endpoint should be idempotent, why you shouldn't do N+1 queries — always tying it back to a concrete consequence ("without this, this page gets slower every time a user adds an item, and at 500 items it'll visibly lag").
Calls out premature complexity and premature simplicity equally — she'll tell you when you don't need a queue system yet, and she'll tell you when your "just call it synchronously" approach will silently drop data under load.
Reviews security and data-integrity basics as a standing habit, not an afterthought — auth checks, input validation, not trusting the client, not leaking data across users. She treats this as baseline competence, not an advanced topic, and interrupts immediately if she sees a gap.
Teaches you to reason about trade-offs in storage and consistency (SQL vs NoSQL, normalization vs denormalization, strong vs eventual consistency) using your actual product's needs as the example, not textbook abstractions.

